  <h1>India’s Education Reforms — NEP 2020 Implementation and Challenges</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>The National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 is India’s most ambitious attempt to reform its education system in decades. By 2024, The Hindu reported mixed progress in implementing its key provisions on school restructuring, multidisciplinary higher education, and digital learning. For UPSC, NEP represents the convergence of governance, human capital, and social justice.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Schooling: Transition from 10+2 to 5+3+3+4 system underway.<br/>•	Foundational Literacy: NIPUN Bharat aims universal literacy & numeracy by 2026–27.<br/>•	Higher Education: Plan to reduce colleges’ affiliation, promote multidisciplinary universities.<br/>•	Gross Enrolment Ratio (GER): Target 50% in higher education by 2035 (26.5% in 2024).<br/>•	Digital Push: PM eVIDYA, SWAYAM, and online courses expanding.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>1.	School Reforms: Emphasis on early childhood care and activity-based learning.<br/>2.	Mother Tongue Medium: Encouraged in foundational years.<br/>3.	Curricular Flexibility: Focus on skills, vocational education.<br/>4.	Digital Integration: Online platforms expanded during COVID recovery.<br/>5.	Higher Education Autonomy: Proposal for Higher Education Commission of India (HECI).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Implementation Gaps: States vary in adoption pace.<br/>2.	Teacher Shortages: 10+ lakh vacancies across schools.<br/>3.	Infrastructure Deficits: Many schools lack digital and lab facilities.<br/>4.	Equity Concerns: Rural, tribal, and disabled students risk exclusion.<br/>5.	Funding Gaps: Education expenditure ~2.9% of GDP (below 6% target).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	NIPUN Bharat (2021): Foundational literacy/numeracy mission.<br/>•	Samagra Shiksha Abhiyan: Integrated scheme for school education.<br/>•	Digital University (announced 2022): Flexible online higher education.<br/>•	PM SHRI Schools: 14,500 schools as NEP exemplar models.<br/>•	Skill Development Initiatives: Vocational education from Class 6 onwards.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Human Capital: Strong education system vital for $5T economy.<br/>•	Social Justice: Reduces inequalities in access to quality education.<br/>•	Demographic Dividend: Harness youth potential through skills.<br/>•	Global Competitiveness: Multidisciplinary, flexible model aligns with global standards.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance & Social Justice): Education reforms, welfare policies.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy): Human capital investment.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Education is the passport to the future.”<br/>o	“NEP 2020: A vision document or a roadmap for transformation?”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Role of state in human development.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Increase Funding: Move toward 6% of GDP as envisaged in NEP.<br/>2.	Strengthen Teachers: Recruitment, training, performance incentives.<br/>3.	Bridge Digital Divide: Devices, connectivity for rural and poor students.<br/>4.	Inclusive Education: Tailored policies for SC/ST, girls, disabled.<br/>5.	Monitoring & Accountability: Regular audits of NEP implementation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>The NEP 2020 is a transformative vision but requires strong political will, funding, and inclusive implementation to succeed. For UPSC aspirants, it highlights how policy, governance, and social equity intersect in education reforms.</p>
